get the right answer without  actually knowing why it's right and so  to that point i want to say briefly  don't write  trick distractor answers especially  those where you haven't actually taught  or explained to learners  why the incorrect answer would be  incorrect you want to make sure to test  learners only on skills that you've  shown them so far  otherwise it's a waste of time for them  and for you  so now that we've talked about how to  implement  assessment formatively during a lesson  to check learners understanding  we're now ready to build the lesson  itself so the nice thing about backwards  design is that once you've spelled out  your learning objectives  and your check-in tests ideally the  lesson itself is almost done  once you've laid out your assessments  you should be able to see exactly what  you need to demonstrate and explain  in order to equip learners with the  knowledge necessary to complete the  check-ins that you've laid out  so the core principle that i try to rely  on when crafting lessons  is to teach what you need to teach no  more  and no less this is gone abroad and  covers a lot of things so i'm going to  break it down for you  first it's important to calibrate to  learners incoming skill level  what do you think that learners should  be able to do already before they start  your lesson  start where learners already are so  don't re-teach stuff if you don't have  to  and teach everything that learners need  to know to hit your objective and this  means that you need to think through all  of the prereqs and make sure that either  you think learners already know them and  you check it to be sure  and that you're teaching everything else  next  allow your formative assessment  check-ins to break your lesson into  manageable chunks  if you have implemented the right number  and the right scope of these check-ins  each of these check-ins should serve as  the end to a bite-sized piece of lesson  about five  no more than 10 minutes and these bite  sizes or these bytes are a good size for  you  because you get to take a break from  talking and you get to check everyone's  progress  and they're a good size for learners  because they get to take a breather from  new information so that they can  solidify  what they've just learned and practice  it with the check-in  next teach less than you think you have  time for  an instructor teaching a lesson for the  first time is  often likely to find that that lesson  just takes longer than you originally  planned  and running out of time before you  finish pretty much 100  guarantees that your learners aren't  going to reach the learning objective  so finishing your content in time is key  and that doesn't mean go too fast  generally it means plan for less time  and let things go longer  i usually plan for about two-thirds of  the time that i  know that i'm gonna have when i time my  lesson out beforehand if i've never  taught it before  and then it usually comes out right but  your methods may vary  and next teach one way to do things  one of the great and terrible features  of r is that there's often  many different ways to code a particular  problem while some of these ways may be  more verbose or less generalizable than  others  sometimes you just get to two ways that  really differ just on personal taste  for example in the pivot longer chunk  that i showed before  where we're teaching people how to pivot  the iris data set  there's two different ways to split the  column names  one way is that we could use the names  sep under argument  of iris in order to break up column  names at the period  another way is that we could use names  pattern to use capture groups to break  up the two  sections of the column name so in  practice there are valid arguments for  using either one of these methods  using name sep to split as you can see  is less for both  but theoretically is less flexible than  if we were to use capture groups with  names  pattern just in case there are future  columns that don't all have  the period for example here we have to  think about  what is the learning goal if the primary  objective  is to learn about the two different  arguments that you can use with pivot  longer  then definitely show the two and talk  about the similarities and differences  between each strategy  but if the objective is only to be able  to pivot the data  at all and not necessarily to be able to  tell the difference between these two  specifications  then teach only the way that you want  students to be able  to do if comparing and contrasting  between two different coding methods  isn't part of your objective pick the  one you'd rather students be able to do  and stick with it that'll be much less  confusing to students and it'll save  you time so  now that we have talked about building  the lesson you're now ready to  deliver it so most likely you're gonna  teach  uh via live coding whether it be  standing up in front of a group of  people  and coding along with your computer  projected to a screen  um or a screen shared out to a large  group or if you're just one-on-one  coding on your computer next to someone  who's got say their laptop next to you  you demonstrate the code example on your  machine and then learners  follow along with what you've just done  so coding for an audience is a little  different than coding for yourself  you have to be intentional even if the  audience is just one person with their  computer next to yours  intentional live coding can spell the  difference between them following along  with you  versus not the single biggest tip i can  give  for any live coding context is to  narrate  everything that you click or type like  literally  everything every word every parenthesis  every quotation mark every hotkey  shortcut if you're clicking  say what you're clicking where it is on  screen spell it out  this helps in many respects for example  it helps learners orient themselves to  where they are in the r studio or  whatever other coding window that you're  using  especially if they're not super familiar  with like where menus or panes or  buttons are  um you spelling things out helps them  find things faster  and next it helps slow your pace down as  you're typing  so that learners are better able to  actually keep up with you because  more novice learners tend to just type  code a lot slower than more  experienced folks and next it helps  model effective coding technique  for example if you use a certain set of  hotkeys as shortcuts to make your life  easier  you want to pass that knowledge along to  learners as well and  if you make typos while you're coding if  you can narrate out  how you catch and fix those typos it  helps learners with a big implicit  objective of learning to code  which is catching their own mistakes and  i do want to note that with live coding  of course remember the lesson building  tip from before  to calibrate to your learner's existing  skill level um  if you know that your learners for  example already know where the help tab  is in our studio  you don't have to say you're going over  to the bottom right to click the help  tab if you do go over there  you can just say i'm going to the docs  and then go and click  so you can calibrate how much you  narrate to how much experience your  learners already have  but in general it doesn't hurt to err on  the side of narrating a little extra  so finally i'd like to go back over the  objectives  of our talk or of my talk now that we've  gone through everything  so by now i hope you are able to compare  principles of backward lesson design  to principles of coding apply that  design to outline in our lesson  from objectives to tests to content and  more specifically be able to write and  implement  these short check-ins to check learner  understanding and finally  be able to describe some benefits of  slow  narrated live coding so  with that knowledge thank you for your  attention i hope you go forth and feel  more equipped to teach effectively thank  you
 

